From 5b2f227e6d1510a859436ad0169596a3de3f72df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shircomay <112399495+shircomay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 23:22:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] feat(audio): capture host timestamps for mic and
 app-audio buffers

Meeting mode pairs the microphone and the app audio by arrival order.
ScreenCaptureKit delivers nothing while the app is silent, so the offset
between the two streams jumps every time the far end goes quiet; an echo
canceller cannot follow that. It needs to know when each buffer was
captured, on one clock.

The recorders' AsyncStreams carry no per-buffer metadata and are shared with
code that should not change, so the timestamp travels beside the buffer in a
small bounded FIFO, CaptureTimestampQueue: the capture callback pushes one
entry right before it yields a buffer, the consumer pops one per buffer it
receives. The queue is disabled by default (push is a no-op), so recordings
that do not use it behave exactly as before.

- MicrophoneRecorder: `timestamps` filled from the tap's AVAudioTime, and a
  `deferGainToConsumer` switch. Adaptive gain that moves underneath an
  adaptive echo filter looks like a changing echo path, so when the switch is
  on the tap leaves the gain to the consumer, which applies it after the
  canceller. It is read at every bring-up, rebuilds included.
- SystemAudioRecorder: `timestamps` filled from the sample buffer's
  presentation time; `deliver` / `deliverForTesting` gain a defaulted
  `hostTimeSeconds` so existing callers compile unchanged.

Nothing consumes the queues yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
